RE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';`\n\n## Scans the query forces\n\n1. **Full scan of `users`.** `lower(u.email)` applies a function to the column, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The planner has to read every user row and lowercase each email. This is the main cost.\n2. **Full scan of `orders` (or a hash join that reads it all), unless `orders.user_id` is indexed.** After the one matching user is found, each of their orders needs to be located. Without an index on `orders.user_id`, that means scanning the whole table or building a hash over it.\n3. **Wide row reads from `SELECT *`.** This pulls every column from both tables, including duplicate `id` columns. It also prevents index-only scans and moves more data than the caller probably needs.\n\n## Fixes\n\n**1. Index the expression.** This works in Postgres, and SQLite supports it too. MySQL 8.0.13+ uses functional indexes with double parentheses.\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n```\nThis turns scan 1 into an index lookup. Use `UNIQUE` if emails are unique case-insensitively.\n\n**2. Index the join key.**\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\nThis turns scan 2 into an index lookup of that user's orders.\n\n**3. Alternative: normalize the data.** If you can store emails already lowercased, or use `citext` (Postgres) or a case-insensitive collation, you can write `WHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'` against a plain unique index on `email`. This avoids the expression index and keeps the predicate simple.\n\n**4. Select only the columns you need.**\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\nAdjust the column list to what the caller uses. A narrower list also lets you use a covering index on `orders (user_id) INCLUDE (...)`.\n\n## Expected result\nWith indexes 1 and 2, the plan should be an index lookup on `users` that returns one row, followed by an index lookup on `orders` for that user. Check with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` to confirm no `Seq Scan` remains on either table. On a small table the planner may still choose a seq scan, so test on realistic data volumes.\n\nI haven't run this against a database. The recommendations come from reading the query, and I don't know your engine, schema or row counts.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}